---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import MoreInCategory from "../components/MoreInCategory.astro";
import { authorSchema, authorName } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import QuickPicks from "../components/QuickPicks.astro";
import PlanTheJob from "../components/PlanTheJob.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { amazonLink } from "../data/affiliate";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";

const moreInCategory = [
  {
    "title": "Best Portable Generators",
    "url": "/best-portable-generator/"
  },
  {
    "title": "How Much Does a Generator Cost?",
    "url": "/how-much-does-a-generator-cost/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Propane Generators",
    "url": "/best-propane-generator/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Dual Fuel Generators",
    "url": "/best-dual-fuel-generator/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Portable vs Standby Generator",
    "url": "/portable-vs-standby-generator/"
  }
];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-11-05", modified: "2026-11-05" });
const title = "5 Best Diesel Generators in 2026: Portable and Standby, Ranked";
const description =
  "The best diesel generators on Amazon US, portable and standby, ranked on runtime, fuel storage and real cost, plus who should skip diesel and buy gas instead.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    "id": "generac-xd5000e-6864",
    "name": "Generac XD5000E 6864 Diesel Portable Generator",
    "category": "Air-Cooled Portable Diesel, 5,000 Running Watts",
    "badge": "Best Portable Diesel Generator",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Generac rates it at 5,000 running and 5,500 starting watts at 120/240V",
      "12 gallon tank rated for 32.4 hours at 50 percent load",
      "Yanmar LW Series 435cc air-cooled direct-injection diesel",
      "L14-30R 240V twist-lock, L5-30R and two 20A GFCI duplexes",
      "Idle control and an hour meter for service intervals"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Around 250 lb, and the wheel kit is sold separately",
      "Roughly four times the price of a gas generator of the same output",
      "Owners consistently describe it as loud"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The only mainstream-brand portable diesel on Amazon US, and the one to buy if you want diesel's runtime and fuel storage in something you can still wheel out of a shed.",
    "description": "The XD5000E is the portable diesel most buyers end up at, because it is the one built by a major generator brand with a national dealer and parts network. Generac rates it at 5,000 running and 5,500 starting watts at 120/240 volts, which puts it in the same output class as a mid-size gasoline open-frame. Power comes from a Yanmar LW Series 435cc air-cooled, direct-injection diesel, a commercial engine family with a long service record on jobsites. The headline number is runtime: Generac lists 32.4 hours at 50 percent load from the 12 gallon tank, which works out to well under half a gallon an hour while delivering about 2,500 watts. The spec sheet lists two 120V 20A GFCI duplex outlets, a 120V L5-30R twist-lock and a 120/240V L14-30R, so it can feed a manual transfer switch or inlet box with a standard 30 amp four-prong cord. Generac quotes total harmonic distortion under 6 percent (some retailer listings say under 5), idle control to cut fuel and noise when loads drop, and an hour meter. It starts electrically, but retailer listings note the battery is not included, and the wheel kit is an extra, which matters on a machine Generac's spec sheets put at around 247 to 252 lb. The trade-off is price: Generac's site lists it from about $4,019 and Amazon listings have shown it around $4,249, against well under $1,000 for a gasoline 5,000-watt unit. It is also a loud machine by owner accounts. Buy it if you already keep diesel on the property or need days of runtime; otherwise a gas or dual fuel unit does the same job for a fraction of the money."
  },
  {
    "id": "energypac-ml6yyade-ep",
    "name": "Energypac Systems ML6YYADE-EP 6kW Diesel Generator",
    "category": "Air-Cooled Open-Frame Diesel, Yanmar L100",
    "badge": "Best Open-Frame Diesel for Farms",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Yanmar L100 9.3 HP air-cooled industrial diesel",
      "Electric start with recoil backup, battery included",
      "120V 30A twist-lock, 240V 20A twist-lock, 120V duplex and 12V DC charging",
      "Automatic voltage regulator and low-oil shutdown",
      "Listing states each unit is shop tested"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "3.5 gallon tank is listed at about 8 hours, far short of the Generac",
      "About 320 lb on a roll-bar skid with no wheels",
      "Costs more than the Generac for a similar output"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A simple, rugged Yanmar-powered open-frame for a farm or shop that already has diesel on site, but at this price most home buyers should take the Generac.",
    "description": "Energypac Systems is the generator line of M&L Engine, a Louisiana company that has distributed Isuzu industrial engines since the 1970s, and the ML6YYADE-EP is its smallest diesel on Amazon. It is a straightforward open-frame machine built around a Yanmar L100, which the listing describes as a 9.3 HP, 3,600 rpm, air-cooled diesel with electric start and a recoil backup. Yanmar's L-series single-cylinder engines are among the most common small diesels in the world, which matters for parts years from now. The listing rates it at 6 kW and lists a 120V duplex, a 120V 30 amp twist-lock, a 240V 20 amp twist-lock, and a 12V DC receptacle rated at 8.3 amps for battery charging, along with an automatic voltage regulator, key switch and low-oil shutdown. The battery and fuel and oil filters are included, and the listing says each unit is shop tested before it ships. Two things count against it. The tank is only 3.5 gallons, which the listing puts at about 8 hours, so for multi-day outages you are refuelling three or four times a day or plumbing in an external tank. And it sits on a roll-bar skid at roughly 320 lb according to the listing, so it is meant to be set in place with a tractor or forklift, not wheeled around. Note too that the 240V outlet is a 20 amp twist-lock rather than the 30 amp L14-30R most home inlet boxes expect, so check your connection plan. Amazon listings have shown it at nearly $5,900, which is more than the Generac. It makes sense for a farm, a remote shop or a hunting property that runs on diesel already and values a common Yanmar engine; for a suburban house, it is not the better buy."
  },
  {
    "id": "energypac-ml9ihrd-t4-ep",
    "name": "Energypac Systems ML9IHRD-T4-EP 9kW Enclosed Diesel Generator",
    "category": "Liquid-Cooled Enclosed Diesel, Isuzu 3CH1, 1,800 rpm",
    "badge": "Best Diesel Standby for Home",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Isuzu 3CH1 three-cylinder diesel running at 1,800 rpm",
      "Level 2 sound-attenuated enclosure with lockable doors",
      "Auto/manual start control panel with safety shutdowns",
      "14 gallon sub-base tank with level gauge",
      "Two NEMA 14-50R 50 amp outlets plus two 120V duplexes"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Close to 1,000 lb: a pad-mounted machine, not a portable",
      "Needs an automatic transfer switch and an electrician to use auto start",
      "Priced above many installed gas standby units"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The diesel to buy if you want a permanently sited, slow-turning backup for a house in an area without natural gas, and are prepared to pay for it.",
    "description": "The ML9IHRD-T4-EP is a different kind of machine from the two portables above. It is a liquid-cooled, three-cylinder Isuzu 3CH1 diesel that turns at 1,800 rpm, half the speed of an air-cooled portable, inside what the listing calls a Level 2 sound-attenuated, powder-coated enclosure with lockable doors. Slow, liquid-cooled engines are why diesel standby sets are the default for hospitals and cell towers: they run cooler, quieter and longer between overhauls than small air-cooled engines. The listing rates it at 9 kW and describes a heavy-duty marine-coated brushless generator end with an automatic voltage regulator, a safety shutdown system, a residential muffler, and a 14 gallon sub-base fuel tank with a level gauge. The control panel is auto/manual start, which means it can be wired to an automatic transfer switch that starts it when the grid fails, but that switch and the electrician to install it are separate costs. For a temporary connection the panel also carries two 120V duplex outlets, two NEMA 14-50R 50 amp receptacles, and a main full-power receptacle with a matching plug. At roughly 996 lb by the listing's weight, it goes on a pad and stays there. Amazon listings have shown it around $10,700 before installation, which is more than many installed gas standby units of similar size; our whole house generator guide covers those. It is the right buy for a rural house without natural gas whose owner wants diesel's storage life and a machine that can run for days, not for anyone who could pipe in gas."
  },
  {
    "id": "energypac-ml15yerd36-ep",
    "name": "Energypac Systems ML15YERD36-EP 15kW Enclosed Diesel Generator",
    "category": "Enclosed Diesel Standby, 15 kW",
    "badge": "Most Diesel kW per Dollar",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Rated at 15 kW, enough for a typical well pump, furnace and central AC circuit",
      "Auto/manual start panel with low-oil and high-temperature shutdown",
      "Level 2 sound-attenuated enclosure with lockable doors",
      "Vibration isolators and an industrial air cleaner",
      "Two NEMA 14-50R outlets and a main full-power receptacle"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "The Amazon listing does not name the engine; ask the seller before buying",
      "14 gallon tank is small for a 15 kW set at heavy load",
      "About 890 lb and needs professional installation"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The most diesel standby output per dollar on Amazon, but confirm the engine model and rated fuel burn with M&L before you order.",
    "description": "The ML15YERD36-EP is listed at only a few hundred dollars more than the 9 kW set, with 15 kW of rated output, which makes it the strongest value on paper among the enclosed diesels on Amazon. The listing describes an auto/manual start control panel with a key switch and safety shutdown for low engine oil and high temperature, 12 volt electrics, vibration isolators, an industrial air cleaner, a residential muffler, engine support legs, and oil and fuel filters. Like the 9 kW model it uses a 14 gallon sub-base fuel tank with a level gauge, a centre-point lifting eye, and a Level 2 sound-attenuated, powder-coated enclosure with lockable doors. The outlet panel carries a circuit breaker, two 120V duplexes, two NEMA 14-50R 50 amp receptacles and a main full-power receptacle. Fifteen kilowatts is enough to run a well pump, a gas or oil furnace blower, a refrigerator and freezer, lighting and, depending on the unit, a central air conditioner, which puts it in the size range most whole-house standby buyers land in. Our concern is information, not hardware: the Amazon listing does not name the engine or publish a fuel consumption figure, and with a 14 gallon tank on a 15 kW set, runtime at heavy load will be far shorter than the bigger 80 gallon unit below. M&L Engine sells Yanmar- and Isuzu-powered sets, so ask which engine and which fuel burn figures apply before you buy. Amazon has listed it around $11,300 and the listing puts the weight at roughly 890 lb. It is a good-value machine for a buyer who will confirm those details first, and a risky one for anyone who will not."
  },
  {
    "id": "energypac-ml14ihrd-t4-ep",
    "name": "Energypac Systems ML14IHRD-T4-EP 14kW Enclosed Diesel Generator",
    "category": "Liquid-Cooled Diesel Standby, Isuzu 3CE1, 80 gal Tank",
    "badge": "Longest Runtime Between Refills",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Isuzu 3CE1 three-cylinder diesel at 1,800 rpm",
      "80 gallon welded aluminium fuel tank with level gauge",
      "Automatic start panel with LCD engine and generator monitoring",
      "Oversized generator end for full single-phase or three-phase output",
      "Aluminium sound-attenuated enclosure, fork pockets and lifting eye"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "The most expensive generator here by thousands of dollars",
      "About 1,380 lb; plan for a crane, forklift or tractor to place it",
      "Overkill for a house that could use natural gas"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The pick for a remote property that needs a week of autonomy without a fuel delivery, and the clearest example of what a true diesel standby looks like.",
    "description": "The ML14IHRD-T4-EP is the closest thing on Amazon to a commercial diesel standby set scaled for a large house or a farm. The listing describes an Isuzu 3CE1 three-cylinder diesel running at 1,800 rpm, a heavy-duty brushless generator end with marine coating and an automatic voltage regulator, and an oversized generator end so it can deliver its full rated output on either single-phase or three-phase power, which matters on farms with three-phase equipment. What sets it apart is the fuel system: an 80 gallon welded aluminium tank with a level gauge, more than five times the capacity of the 9 and 15 kW sets. That is the difference between refuelling daily and running for several days at moderate load between top-ups, though M&L does not publish a runtime figure in the listing, so do the maths from the engine's rated consumption once you have it. The control panel is automatic start with an LCD showing engine data, amps, volts, hertz and fuel level, so it is designed to be wired to an automatic transfer switch. It sits in a Level 2 sound-attenuated, powder-coated aluminium enclosure with lockable doors, on a welded steel skid with fork pockets and a single lifting eye. At about 1,380 lb according to the listing, it is placed with machinery and set on a pad. Amazon has listed it around $17,600, before the transfer switch, pad, wiring and permit, so the installed cost runs well past $20,000. That is a lot of money, and for a house on a natural gas line a gas standby is cheaper and simpler. For a ranch, a remote cabin with a long winter road or a property with three-phase loads, it is exactly the kind of machine diesel exists to be."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Diesel Generator",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-diesel-generator/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is a diesel generator better than a gas generator?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For long runtimes and on-site fuel storage, yes: diesel burns less fuel per kilowatt-hour at load and stores much longer than gasoline. For most homes, no. Diesel portables cost roughly four times as much as gas units of the same output, weigh more and are louder. Unless you keep diesel on site or need days of runtime, gas or dual fuel is the better buy."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long can diesel fuel be stored for a generator?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Untreated diesel is commonly cited at six to twelve months in a clean, sealed tank, and longer with a stabiliser and biocide. Gasoline is usually cited at three to six months untreated. Keep diesel tanks full and dry, drain water periodically, and rotate the fuel into vehicles or equipment yearly so it is always fresh."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I run a diesel generator on heating oil or off-road diesel?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Dyed off-road diesel is commonly used in generators, since they are off-road equipment, and it is usually cheaper because it is not taxed for highway use. Heating oil is chemically similar but is not made to the same specification for engines, so check the engine maker's fuel requirements before using it. Never use off-road diesel in a road vehicle."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is wet stacking in a diesel generator?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Wet stacking is the build-up of unburned fuel and soot in the exhaust when a diesel runs at light load for long periods and never gets hot enough to burn its fuel completely. Signs include black, oily residue at the exhaust and falling power. Avoid it by sizing the generator close to your load and running it under heavier load regularly."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long will a portable diesel generator run?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It depends on the tank and load. Generac rates the XD5000E at 32.4 hours on its 12 gallon tank at 50 percent load, which is unusually long for a portable. The Energypac 6 kW open-frame has a 3.5 gallon tank listed at about 8 hours. Enclosed standby sets with large base tanks can run for days at moderate load."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Are diesel generators loud?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Air-cooled portable diesels generally are. Owners commonly describe the Generac XD5000E as one of the louder portables they have used, with the knocking note typical of a single-cylinder diesel. Enclosed, liquid-cooled diesel sets turning at 1,800 rpm inside sound-attenuated cabinets are much quieter, which is a large part of why they cost so much more."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can a diesel generator power a whole house?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A 5,000 to 6,000 watt portable diesel covers essentials such as a fridge, freezer, furnace blower, well pump on 240V and lights, but not central air and an electric range together. A 14 or 15 kW enclosed diesel with an automatic transfer switch can run most of a typical house, including a central air conditioner in many cases, depending on its size."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: authorName(Astro.url.pathname),
    section: "Home Emergency Preparedness",
    tags: [
      "diesel generator",
      "generator diesel generator",
      "portable diesel generator",
      "diesel generator for home",
      "diesel generator for backup power"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10" style="background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 20% 20%, #38BDF8 0, transparent 45%), radial-gradient(circle at 80% 60%, #FF4500 0, transparent 40%);"></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated November 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          The Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Diesel Generators</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          Five real diesel generators sold on Amazon US, from a 250 lb portable to a 1,380 lb standby with an 80 gallon tank, and an honest look at who actually needs one.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#generac-xd5000e-6864"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#top-picks"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            See All 5 Picks
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="7.6k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Our 5 Diesel Picks Reviewed</a>
          <a href="#who-benefits" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Who Benefits From Diesel</a>
          <a href="#fuel-storage" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Fuel Storage vs Gasoline</a>
          <a href="#efficiency" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Fuel Burn and Runtime</a>
          <a href="#wet-stacking" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Wet Stacking</a>
          <a href="#downsides" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Noise, Weight and Cold</a>
          <a href="#air-vs-liquid" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Portable vs Standby Diesel</a>
          <a href="#hookup" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Connecting to the House</a>
          <a href="#alternatives" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. When Gas Is Better</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A diesel generator is the right buy if you keep diesel on the property, need days of runtime, or want a standby set where there is no gas line. For everyone else it is a louder, heavier machine at about four times the price of a gas generator, and the honest recommendation is to skip it.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />


        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The 5 Best Diesel Generators, Ranked
          </h2>

          <p>
            Real diesel generators are thin on the ground at mainstream retailers, so this list is short on purpose. We looked for diesel units sold on Amazon US with exact model numbers and checked each against the manufacturer's spec sheet or the seller's published listing for rated output, engine, fuel tank, runtime, outlets, start type and weight. All figures are manufacturer or listing ratings, not our measurements, and where a listing leaves something out, such as the engine or a runtime figure, we say so rather than guess. Prices are Amazon listing prices at the time of writing and change often.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <div id={product.id} class="scroll-mt-24 mt-12 first:mt-8">
              <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-sm hover:shadow-md transition-shadow">
                <div class="bg-gray-900 px-6 py-4 flex items-center justify-between">
                  <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
                    <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black">
                      {index + 1}
                    </span>
                    {productImages[product.id] && (
                      <img
                        src={productImages[product.id]}
                        alt={product.name}
                        loading="lazy"
                        width="40"
                        height="40"
                        class="w-10 h-10 rounded-lg bg-white object-contain p-1 shrink-0"
                      />
                    )}
                    <div>
                      <h3 class="text-white font-black text-lg leading-snug m-0"><span class="block text-[#38BDF8] text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wide mb-1 sm:hidden">{product.badge}</span>{product.name}</h3>
                      <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs">{product.category}</p>
                    </div>
                  </div>
