        <div id="wet-stacking" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Wet Stacking: The Light-Load Problem Nobody Mentions
          </h2>

          <p>
            Wet stacking is the diesel downside that catches home buyers out, because it is caused by exactly the way most people run a backup generator. A diesel engine needs heat and cylinder pressure to burn its fuel completely. Run it at a small fraction of its rated output for hours on end and it never gets hot enough. Unburned fuel and soot pass into the exhaust, collect as a black, oily residue, and glaze the cylinder walls.
          </p>

          <p>
            The symptoms are a wet, black exhaust stack, dripping residue around the muffler joints, falling power and rising oil consumption. Left long enough, it can foul injectors and damage the engine. Generator service technicians generally advise against running a diesel below roughly 30 to 40 percent of its rating for long periods, and many recommend periodic runs at higher load to burn the deposits off.
          </p>

          <p>
            This is why sizing matters more for diesel than for gasoline. A 15 kW diesel set that spends most outages powering 2 kW of refrigerators and lights is a wet-stacking machine. If you want a diesel, size it close to your real running load, not your worst-case dream load. Our <a href="/what-size-generator-do-i-need-for-my-house/">generator sizing guide</a> walks through adding up what you actually need running at once.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you already own an oversized diesel, there are workable fixes. Add load during long runs, for example a water heater or space heater circuit, so the engine works harder for an hour or two each day. Exercise it monthly at a meaningful load rather than idling it. Commercial users bring in a load bank for annual testing; homeowners can get most of the benefit by planning their exercise runs around real loads.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">SIZING RULE FOR DIESEL</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Aim for a typical load of at least half the generator's rating.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Gasoline engines tolerate an oversized generator. Diesels punish it. If your normal outage load is 2,000 watts, a 5,000-watt diesel is a reasonable fit; a 15 kW set is not.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="downsides" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Noise, Weight, Cold Starts and Price
          </h2>

          <p>
            The remaining downsides are less subtle. Air-cooled portable diesels are loud. Generac does not lead with a decibel figure for the XD5000E, and owners commonly describe it as one of the louder portables they have run, with the hard, knocking note typical of a single-cylinder diesel. If neighbours are close, the noise is a real cost; our <a href="/best-quiet-generator/">quiet generator guide</a> is the opposite end of the spectrum. Enclosed liquid-cooled sets running at 1,800 rpm are much quieter, which is a big part of their price.
          </p>

          <p>
            They are also heavy. The Generac is around 250 lb, and the Energypac open-frame is around 320 lb on a skid with no wheels. A gasoline generator of the same output typically weighs 150 to 200 lb. The enclosed standby units range from about 890 to 1,380 lb and are placed with machinery. If you need to move a generator in and out of a garage on your own, diesel is a hard sell.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cold weather is the third issue. Diesel relies on compression heat to ignite, so cold engines crank harder, and small air-cooled diesels are less forgiving than a modern gasoline engine on a January morning. Fuel is the bigger concern: summer-grade No. 2 diesel can cloud and gel at low temperatures, plugging filters. In cold climates, buy winter-blended fuel or treat stored fuel with an anti-gel additive before the cold sets in, and keep the starting battery charged.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then there is price and choice. A 5,000-watt gasoline open-frame typically sells for well under $1,000; the diesel XD5000E is listed at over $4,000. Residential diesel options are thin: Generac's portable is the main big-brand model sold on Amazon US, and most of the rest are industrial sets sold through dealers. The <a href="/how-much-does-a-generator-cost/">generator cost guide</a> lays out what each fuel type typically costs, and diesel sits at the top of every range.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="air-vs-liquid" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Air-Cooled Portable vs Liquid-Cooled Standby Diesel
          </h2>

          <p>
            The diesels on this list fall into two classes that have little in common beyond the fuel. Air-cooled portables, like the Generac and the open-frame Energypac, use a single-cylinder engine spinning at 3,600 rpm to produce 60 Hz power directly. They are relatively compact, can be moved, plug into a transfer switch or inlet with a cord, and cost a few thousand dollars.
          </p>

          <p>
            Liquid-cooled standby sets, like the three enclosed Energypac units, use multi-cylinder engines turning at 1,800 rpm with a radiator, inside a sound-attenuated enclosure on a skid with a fuel tank in the base. Half the engine speed means less wear, less noise and a much longer service life between overhauls. They are designed to be wired to an automatic transfer switch and start themselves when the grid fails.
          </p>

          <p>
            The choice comes down to how you will use it. If you want something to wheel out a few times a year, run with cords or through a manual transfer switch, and occasionally take to a jobsite, an air-cooled portable is the right class. If you want a permanent backup that runs a well, a furnace and a whole farmhouse for days without you doing anything, the liquid-cooled set is the real thing and the portable is not.
          </p>

          <p>
            The cost difference is large. The portables here are listed at roughly $4,000 to $6,000. The enclosed sets are listed at roughly $10,700 to $17,600 before the pad, transfer switch, wiring and permit, which can add several thousand more. For comparison, our <a href="/whole-house-generator-installation-cost/">whole house generator installation cost</a> guide shows what those installation items typically add to a gas standby, and the same line items apply to diesel.
          </p>

