        <div id="hookup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Connecting a Diesel Generator to Your House
          </h2>

          <p>
            A portable diesel connects exactly like a gasoline portable. The Generac's L14-30R outlet takes a standard four-prong 30 amp generator cord, which runs to a power inlet box on the outside wall, which in turn feeds either a manual transfer switch or an interlock kit on your main panel. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-hook-up-a-generator-to-your-house/">hooking up a generator to your house</a> covers the three legal ways to do it, and our <a href="/best-generator-inlet-box/">generator inlet box</a> guide covers the hardware.
          </p>

          <p>
            The Energypac open-frame is a slightly awkward fit for that setup, because its 240V outlet is listed as a 20 amp twist-lock rather than the 30 amp L14-30R that most inlet boxes are built around. That is solvable with the right inlet and cord, but it is worth sorting out before the outage, not during it. Never backfeed a house through a dryer outlet with a double-ended cord; it is illegal, it can kill a lineworker, and it can burn the house down.
          </p>

          <p>
            The enclosed standby sets are a professional install. Auto start only works with an automatic transfer switch that signals the generator to start and moves the house load over once the generator is stable. That switch, its installation, the concrete or gravel pad, the wiring run and a permit are all extra. The 50 amp NEMA 14-50R outlets on the 9 and 15 kW sets are useful for running an RV or a shop temporarily, but they are not a substitute for a proper transfer switch on the house.
          </p>

          <p>
            Placement rules apply to every fuel. Keep the generator outdoors and well away from doors, windows and vents, because diesel exhaust carries carbon monoxide like any other engine. Our guide to <a href="/where-to-place-a-generator-outside/">where to place a generator outside</a> covers distances and weather protection, and the same rules apply to a diesel.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="alternatives" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. When Gas, Dual Fuel or Propane Is the Better Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            Be honest about whether you need diesel at all. For a typical suburban house, a dual fuel generator gives you most of diesel's storage advantage, because propane keeps indefinitely in a sealed tank, at a quarter of the price. Our <a href="/best-dual-fuel-generator/">dual fuel generator</a> picks and <a href="/best-5000-watt-generator/">5,000-watt generator</a> picks cover the same output class as the Generac XD5000E for far less money.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you want a permanent, automatic backup and you have natural gas, a gas standby beats a diesel standby on cost, on maintenance, and on fuel logistics, because the utility does the refuelling. Our <a href="/best-whole-house-generator/">whole house generator</a> guide is the place to compare those, and <a href="/portable-vs-standby-generator/">portable vs standby</a> explains the trade-offs between the two classes.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you want something to run quietly at light load near people, diesel is the wrong tool entirely. An inverter generator throttles its engine to match the load, which is both quieter and more efficient at a few hundred watts than any diesel can be. That is also where wet stacking stops being a concern, because there is no diesel to stack.
          </p>

          <p>
            Diesel earns its price in three situations: when it shares a fuel supply with farm equipment, when runtime measured in days matters more than purchase price, and when a slow-turning liquid-cooled standby is wanted where there is no gas line. If you are in one of those, the picks above are the real options on Amazon US. If not, the money is better spent on a good gas or dual fuel generator, a proper inlet and transfer switch, and fuel you actually rotate.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a diesel that is far too big</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Diesels wet stack when they run lightly loaded for long periods. A 15 kW set powering a fridge and lights spends its life soot-fouling its own exhaust. Size a diesel close to your real running load, aim for at least half its rating during a typical outage, and run it under heavier load during monthly exercise.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Storing diesel and forgetting about it</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Diesel keeps much longer than gasoline, but it is not permanent. Water condenses in half-empty tanks and microbes grow at the fuel-water boundary. Keep tanks full, treat with stabiliser and biocide, drain water from the bottom, and rotate stored fuel into a tractor or truck at least once a year.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring winter fuel</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Summer No. 2 diesel can cloud and gel in hard cold, plugging the filter right when you need power. If you live where winters are cold, buy winter-blended fuel in autumn or add an anti-gel treatment to stored fuel before temperatures drop, and keep the starting battery on a maintainer.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming auto start means plug and play</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                An auto/manual start panel on a standby diesel only starts the engine when it receives a signal from an automatic transfer switch. That switch, the electrician, the pad, the wiring and the permit are separate costs that can add several thousand dollars to the listed price.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying diesel for a suburban house with a gas line</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                If natural gas is available, a gas standby is cheaper to buy, install and maintain, and never needs refuelling. If you want a portable, a dual fuel unit with propane storage gets most of diesel's shelf-life benefit for a fraction of the price. Diesel is a specialist fuel for specialist needs.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a diesel generator better than a gas generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For long runtimes and on-site fuel storage, yes: diesel burns less fuel per kilowatt-hour at load and stores much longer than gasoline. For most homes, no. Diesel portables cost roughly four times as much as gas units of the same output, weigh more and are louder. Unless you keep diesel on site or need days of runtime, gas or dual fuel is the better buy.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long can diesel fuel be stored for a generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Untreated diesel is commonly cited at six to twelve months in a clean, sealed tank, and longer with a stabiliser and biocide. Gasoline is usually cited at three to six months untreated. Keep diesel tanks full and dry, drain water periodically, and rotate the fuel into vehicles or equipment yearly so it is always fresh.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I run a diesel generator on heating oil or off-road diesel?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Dyed off-road diesel is commonly used in generators, since they are off-road equipment, and it is usually cheaper because it is not taxed for highway use. Heating oil is chemically similar but is not made to the same specification for engines, so check the engine maker's fuel requirements before using it. Never use off-road diesel in a road vehicle.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is wet stacking in a diesel generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Wet stacking is the build-up of unburned fuel and soot in the exhaust when a diesel runs at light load for long periods and never gets hot enough to burn its fuel completely. Signs include black, oily residue at the exhaust and falling power. Avoid it by sizing the generator close to your load and running it under heavier load regularly.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long will a portable diesel generator run?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the tank and load. Generac rates the XD5000E at 32.4 hours on its 12 gallon tank at 50 percent load, which is unusually long for a portable. The Energypac 6 kW open-frame has a 3.5 gallon tank listed at about 8 hours. Enclosed standby sets with large base tanks can run for days at moderate load.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are diesel generators loud?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Air-cooled portable diesels generally are. Owners commonly describe the Generac XD5000E as one of the louder portables they have used, with the knocking note typical of a single-cylinder diesel. Enclosed, liquid-cooled diesel sets turning at 1,800 rpm inside sound-attenuated cabinets are much quieter, which is a large part of why they cost so much more.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a diesel generator power a whole house?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 5,000 to 6,000 watt portable diesel covers essentials such as a fridge, freezer, furnace blower, well pump on 240V and lights, but not central air and an electric range together. A 14 or 15 kW enclosed diesel with an automatic transfer switch can run most of a typical house, including a central air conditioner in many cases, depending on its size.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

