        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            A <strong class="text-white">diesel generator</strong> is worth its price when you already keep diesel on the property, need days of runtime, or want a slow-turning standby set where there is no gas line. For a portable, the Generac XD5000E is the one to buy: a Yanmar engine, a 240V twist-lock and 32.4 rated hours on a tank. For a permanent backup, the Energypac ML9IHRD-T4-EP is the sensible size and the ML14IHRD-T4-EP is the long-autonomy machine.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            For most suburban homes, though, diesel costs too much and gives too little back. Size any diesel close to your real load so it does not wet stack, treat and rotate its fuel, and if none of the three diesel use cases describes you, buy a good gas or dual fuel generator and spend the difference on a proper inlet and transfer switch.
          </p>
